//
// @IGNORE — DEFECT (both backends, 18594597b): the refusal fires and is followed by a second
//   error, "Unknown variable 'x'", at the body's read of the refused parameter; the file is
//   then failed for an error it does not claim.  The same cascade follows the lone-lambda
//   refusal "Cannot infer type for lambda parameter 'x'" (attic `1067b` and `1926` dodge it by
//   never reading the parameter).
//
// A REFUSED LAMBDA THAT READS ITS PARAMETER SAYS ONLY THE REFUSAL (`@FR-Disp-Hint`): the
// refusal of an untyped `|x|` at a set is the whole answer for that lambda — it names the
// definitions and the cure — and the body's use of `x` is not a second mistake to report.  One
// refusal per file; a second, cascading error makes the file's diagnostics say something the
// program did not get wrong.
//
// @falsified-at: 18594597b — interpret and native: the refusal plus "Unknown variable 'x'".
// CHANNEL: the compile (the expected diagnostic).
// ARMED: no instrument.
// WITNESS: the diagnostic names both members and the typed spelling.
// HOLDS: a typed lambda picks its member (`1926b` in the attic).
// @EXPECT_ERROR: cannot infer the type of lambda parameter 'x': `hl` is hl(fn(integer) -> integer) and hl(fn(text) -> text), so no single definition types the lambda — write its types, `fn(x: <type>) -> <ret> { … }`

fn hl(f: fn(integer) -> integer) -> text { "int {f(2)}" }
fn hl(f: fn(text) -> text) -> text { "text {f("a")}" }

fn main() {
  println(hl(|x| { x }));
}
